# Mid-turn Ctrl+C cancel and recovery. We send a deliberately
# long-running prompt, fire <C-c> while the model is still streaming,
# then issue a different, trivial bash tool call. The second turn
# proves the agent loop recovered: a regression in the cancel +
# reminder-queue path would either leave the second prompt unsent or
# trip up the streaming state machine.
#
# Ctrl+C is the real cancel hotkey in zag (see EventOrchestrator.zig).
# Esc only enters normal mode in the modal keymap; it does NOT abort a
# running agent.

spawn ./zig-out/bin/zag
wait_text /Welcome to zag/
wait_idle 500ms
send "Write a 500-word essay about the history of pseudo-terminals on UNIX. Be thorough." <Enter>
# wait_idle here is intentional: we want the model to START streaming
# before we cancel. There is no deterministic signal for "streaming
# has begun", so a small fixed wait is the honest choice.
wait_idle 4s
send <C-c>
wait_idle 1s
send "Forget that. Just run bash 'echo zag-recover-token-T7'." <Enter>
wait_text /zag-recover-token-T7/ 45s
snapshot final
send "/quit" <Enter>
wait_exit
